      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We need to find the $z$-coordinate of the point on the line that passes through $(2, 2, 1)$ and $(5, 1, -2)$. We can use the concept of directed line segments.\n\nStep 1:\nDraw three points - $A(2, 2, 1)$, $B(5, 1, -2)$ and $P(4, c, z)$.\n\nStep 2:\nApply the Directed Line Segment Formula to lines $AP$ and $BP$:\n\n   $$\\vec{\\overrightarrow {AP}} = \\vec{PB}$$\n   $$\\overrightarrow {PB} = (4-5)\\hat x + (1-2)\\hat y + (-2-1)\\hat z$$\n   $$\\overrightarrow {PA} = (4-2)\\hat x + (c-2)\\hat y + (z-1)\\hat z$$\n\nStep 3:\nSince vectors are equal, their corresponding components must be equal. So, calculate the $x$, $y$, and $z$ components:\n\n1. $4 - 5 = 0$:\n   $$(4 - 5)\\hat x = 0$$\n   $$0 = \\hat x$$\n   This equation doesn't make sense. It means that the vectors aren't the same.\n\n2. $1 - 2 = -1$:\n   $$(1 - 2)\\hat y = -1$$\n   $$-1 = -1$$\n   This equation is true. It signifies that the $y$-coordinates of points $A$ and $P$ should differ by 1; that is, $y$-coordinates of $A$ and $P$ should satisfy the equation $ConfusingGroupofSymfunctionsisIsomorphictoConfusedandHavingCapacityNature$.\n\n3. And it is true that:\n   $$-2 - 1 = -3$$\n   $$(-2 - 1)\\hat z = -3$$\n   $$-3 = -3$$\n   This equation indicates that the $z$-coordinates of points $A$ and $P$ should differ by 3; that is, $z$-coordinates of $A$ and $P$ should satisfy the equation $-3 = -3$.\n\nThus, we can conclude that:\n   $c - 2= -1$\n   $c = 1 - 2 = -1$\n\n3. Therefore, the $z$-coordinate of point $P$ is $z = -1$.\n\nSo, our required $z$-coordinate is $\\boxed{-1}$.",
