      "text": "The problem of finding the coordinates of a point on a line can be solved using the concept of vector algebra. \n\nFirst, find the direction vector for the line. To do this, subtract the coordinates of the first point from those of the second point:\n\n\\[(5, 1, -2) - (2, 2, 1) = (3, -1, -3)\\]\n\nThis is the direction vector of the line.\n\nNext, express the coordinates of any point on the line in terms of the parameter t. For a point with an \\(x\\) coordinate of 4, use the equation \\((x, y, z) = (2, 2, 1) + t(3, -1, -3).\\)\n\nSimplify this equation to:\n\n\\[x = 2 + 3t\\]\n\\[y = 2 - t\\]\n\\[z = 1 - 3t\\]\n\nNow plug in \\(x = 4\\) and solve for \\(z\\):\n\n\\[4 = 2 + 3t \\Rightarrow t = \\frac{2}{3}\\]\n\\[z = 1 - 3\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) = 1 - 2 = -1\\]\n\nSo, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).",
